Myth: Before morning found a path, the world was a dark bowl of water, and silence floated there, round and heavy. Above the waves fluttered Ember-Wren, no larger than a leaf, keeping a coal tucked under her breastbone. The dark breathed to her, “Hide it well. What never begins will never grieve.”

From the deep rose Grandmother Turtle, her shell vast, empty, and shining with salt. “Small fire-bearer,” she said, “I have carried only cold tides. Give me one ember, and I will carry a place where voices may gather.”

Ember-Wren feared losing the coal, for it beat like her own heart. Yet Turtle’s waiting back was lonelier than night. She opened her feathers, and a spark fell. It spread across the shell as red clay, heaved into mountains, folded into valleys, and filled with lakes remembering the sea. Rivers ran laughing. Forests lifted green fingers. Creatures stepped from mud, stone, root, feather, and mist.

Then the dark descended, angry at color. Ember-Wren scattered her hidden fire upward: one spark became sun, one became moon, and countless sparks became stars. Some slept inside wood.

People rose from clay and breath. At every hearth, her courage wakes, while Turtle carries all dawns.
